WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
A closed cylinder contains nitrogen, hydrogen and ammonia,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.440
already at equilibrium.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.760
Nitrogen and hydrogen form ammonia, while ammonia breaks

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.480
down in the reverse direction.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.120
The average opposing rates match.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.280
Now imagine pushing the piston down while keeping

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.560
the temperature constant.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.680
Why does this squeeze lead to more ammonia?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.880
First separate what the piston does from what

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.160
the reaction does.

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:30.326
Pushing the piston down reduces the volume.

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:33.886
The same gas molecules now occupy less space.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.686
Compression itself has not converted nitrogen or hydrogen

00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:38.966
into ammonia.

00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:41.086
It has changed the space available to the

00:00:41.127 --> 00:00:42.166
mixture.

00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:44.806
Temperature is held constant, so this model does

00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:46.966
not show the gas being heated or the

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:49.446
molecules gaining a higher average speed.

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:53.786
Gas pressure comes from molecules hitting the walls.

00:00:54.027 --> 00:00:56.666
With less distance to travel, molecules hit the

00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:58.226
walls more frequently.

00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:00.906
That raises the pressure before the reaction has

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:01.866
changed the mixture.

00:01:02.427 --> 00:01:04.746
The gauge now shows the higher pressure caused

00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:05.626
by compression.

00:01:06.187 --> 00:01:09.066
The cylinder remains closed, so no reacting gas

00:01:09.107 --> 00:01:09.866
has escaped.

00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:12.986
Keep that immediate effect separate from the later

00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:14.586
equilibrium response.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.400
Read the balanced equation.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.480
One nitrogen molecule and three hydrogen molecules can

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.560
form two ammonia molecules.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.760
The small g means gas, so all these

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.240
substances contribute to the amount of gas in

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:29.960
the cylinder.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.000
Count molecules on each side: four on the

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.600
left, two on the right.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.720
The same nitrogen and hydrogen atoms can therefore

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.520
be rearranged into fewer gas molecules.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.013
At constant temperature, compression favours the side with

00:01:45.094 --> 00:01:48.133
fewer gas particles when the gaseous totals differ.

00:01:48.694 --> 00:01:51.813
In this reaction that means the ammonia side.

00:01:52.134 --> 00:01:55.253
A net forward reaction produces more ammonia and

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:57.333
reduces the total amount of gas.

00:01:57.814 --> 00:02:01.173
The animation shows the overall rearrangement, not a

00:02:01.374 --> 00:02:04.933
single four-molecule collision or the detailed reaction pathway.

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:07.973
Matter is conserved even though the number of

00:02:08.054 --> 00:02:09.653
gas molecules changes.

00:02:10.867 --> 00:02:13.026
Now hold the piston in its new position

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:14.866
and keep the temperature fixed.

00:02:15.427 --> 00:02:17.746
The pressure rose immediately when the gas was

00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:18.386
squeezed.

00:02:18.947 --> 00:02:22.066
As more ammonia forms, fewer gas molecules hit

00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:24.946
the walls, so pressure falls from that immediate

00:02:25.027 --> 00:02:25.826
high value.

00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:29.346
The reaction partly offsets the imposed rise.

00:02:29.827 --> 00:02:32.066
It does not undo the compression or put

00:02:32.107 --> 00:02:33.026
the piston back.

00:02:33.587 --> 00:02:36.306
This graph is a schematic trend, not measured

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.266
pressure data.

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:41.346
Try the opposite change in the same nitrogen,

00:02:41.507 --> 00:02:43.106
hydrogen and ammonia mixture.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.026
Start at equilibrium.

00:02:45.507 --> 00:02:48.226
Keep the cylinder closed and the temperature constant.

00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:51.426
Pull the piston up to increase the volume,

00:02:51.547 --> 00:02:52.466
then hold it there.

00:02:53.107 --> 00:02:55.026
What happens to pressure immediately?

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.546
Which reaction direction is favoured afterwards?

00:02:58.787 --> 00:03:00.946
Explain using the balanced equation.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.786
Pause if you need longer.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.240
Pressure falls immediately because the same gas has

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.400
more space.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.920
The reaction then favours the side with more

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.880
gas particles: nitrogen and hydrogen.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.000
Breaking down two ammonia molecules can make one

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.840
nitrogen and three hydrogen molecules.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.840
The increased amount of gas partly offsets the

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:37.560
pressure fall.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.280
The final pressure need not return to its

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.400
original value.
